If you want to specify elements to check with an {@link Iterable}, use {@link #containsExactlyElementsOf(Iterable) containsExactlyElementsOf(Iterable)} instead.

// Assertions will fail:
assertThat(new int[][] { }).isNotEmpty();
assertThat(new int[][] {{ }}).isNotEmpty();
assertThat(new int[][] {{ }, { }, { }}).isNotEmpty(); // Empty because the 2D array contains three empty rows.
int[][] array = null;
assertThat(array).isNotEmpty();

These assertions fail. The first two involve empty 2D arrays, which are considered empty. The third contains three empty rows and no elements. A null array fails as well.

```java
assertThat(new int[][] {}).isNotEmpty();
assertThat(new int[][] {{}}).isNotEmpty();
assertThat(new int[][] {{}, {}, {}}).isNotEmpty();
int[][] array = null;
assertThat(array).isNotEmpty();
```

