Verifies that the actual array is empty. This means the array has no elements; in other words, it can have any number of rows, but all rows must be empty.

<p>

Example:

<pre><code class='java'>
// assertions will pass
assertThat(new int[][] {{}}).isEmpty();

// considered empty because the 2D array comprises three empty rows
assertThat(new int[][] {{ }, { }, { }}).isEmpty();

Verifies that the actual array is empty. Empty means the array has no elements. In other words, it can contain any number of rows, but all rows must be empty.

<p>Example:
<pre><code class='java'>
// assertions will pass
assertThat(new int[][] {{}}).isEmpty();

// This is considered empty because the 2D array contains no elements and consists of three empty rows.
assertThat(new int[][] {{ }, { }, { }}).isEmpty();

given(Byte actual) is a public static method that accepts one Byte parameter named actual and returns an AbstractByteAssert<?> instance. The method signature is:

public static AbstractByteAssert<?> given(Byte actual)

The parameter represents the Byte value supplied to the method. No other parameters or numeric arguments are defined. Its parameter list contains one entry.

